cff-version: 1.2.0
title: >-
  Measuring the Quantum Signature of Relational Time on
  Superconducting Hardware
message: >-
  If you use this software, data, or the experimental protocols
  in this repository, please cite it as below.
type: software
authors:
  - family-names: "O'Neill"
    given-names: "Kevin"
    # orcid: "https://orcid.org/0000-0000-0000-0000"   # add before release
repository-code: "https://github.com/IllI/relational-time-ibm-quantum"
doi: 10.5281/zenodo.21878786
abstract: >-
  An experimental program testing whether relational time -- time defined by
  correlations between a clock and the rest of a system rather than by an
  external parameter -- leaves a measurable quantum signature distinguishable
  from its classical mimic. Fifteen pre-registered runs on IBM Quantum Heron
  processors, 77 jobs, establish in order: that the conditional-evolution
  signal usually cited as "time from entanglement" is reproduced by a
  classical clock control; that the clock-marginal coherence witness is not,
  and has a measured decoherence threshold past which apparent temporal
  dynamics survives its own quantum signature; that this witness is
  nonetheless necessary but not sufficient, since a zero-entanglement coherent
  product state scores higher on it; that no functional of a single local
  product-basis measurement setting can certify clock-system entanglement
  (demonstrated adversarially, then proved); that a multi-setting fidelity
  witness does certify it (F = 0.94/0.88 against a derived bound of 0.5), with
  both adversarial states correctly rejected; that the global state is
  stationary under the paired clock-shift-plus-evolution and under nothing
  else; that detuning the pairing reveals a commensurability condition for
  exact cycle closure of a finite cyclic clock, and superposing the rate
  produces interference no classical rate mixture can; and that entanglement,
  ray-stationarity and internal evolution hold jointly on a single
  preparation. The two closing runs close a class: the marginal witness does
  not merely fail to certify entanglement but runs backwards against CHSH
  (r = -0.9573, the witness-maximising state producing no Bell violation at
  all), and moving a clock's entanglement partner from its own system to a
  second clock trades clock quality against synchronisability at r = -0.9833
  with both endpoints reaching exactly zero. Those are the Jakob-Bergou
  complementarity relation and Coffman-Kundu-Wootters monogamy respectively --
  standard theorems, attributed as such, measured here as continuous hardware
  curves -- and they are one conserved budget, which is why no correlation
  magnitude can serve as a shared temporal reference between two good clocks.
  Includes the preceding cross-datacenter TPU telemetry nulls and the
  synthetic Page-Wootters / D-LinOSS simulation campaign from which the
  hypothesis and the measurement-diversity certification standard originate.
  Two runs are reported as failures on a limitation published unclosed. All
  raw counts, pre-registrations, and server-side provenance are archived;
  every analysis reproduces from counts. Two further runs extend the line to
  two clocks: IBM-13 couples two d=4 clocks each driving its own system and
  measures how much mutual readability they can afford before certification
  fails, and IBM-14 shows the foreign-clock reading certifies nothing by
  dephasing the clock in its computational basis -- entanglement across
  (clock : rest) drops to exactly zero while the measured distribution is
  unchanged, so a provably separable state reproduces the reading at every
  coupling.
keywords:
  - quantum information
  - Page-Wootters mechanism
  - relational time
  - emergent time
  - quantum reference frames
  - entanglement witness
  - decoherence
  - IBM Quantum
  - superconducting qubits
  - pre-registration
  - state-space models
  - D-LinOSS
